// The Lotto benchmark (docs/spec/drift.md, DRIFT-450): samples/lotto.aerie with a system root that
// names the copies of the Lotto repos in tests/unit/extract/fixtures/lotto/, and a code line under
// each element saying which of today's code it stands for. Nothing else differs from the sample, so
// what drift reports is how the sample has drifted from that code.

model lotto: The Lotto suite
  > How results come in, how picks are generated, and what each part does.

audiences exec "Executive", manager "Manager" (default), engineer "Engineer" (code)

system
  repo ../../extract/fixtures/lotto/lotto-options-core
  repo ../../extract/fixtures/lotto/lotto-options-ui
  repo ../../extract/fixtures/lotto/lottery-result-persistence-service
  compose ../../extract/fixtures/lotto/lotto-options-core/compose.yaml

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- elements

user: User | Using a browser [person]
  > Opens the website, picks a state and game, and asks for numbers. The browser only
    ever talks to the website.
  @engineer >> `localhost:4200`; the Screen API, the Analysis engine and the Results store
    are never reached from the browser.

// Unsure: the Results store's own `docker-compose.yml` and Dockerfile, and the root Dockerfile
// of lotto-options-core, run older or partial stacks; the README points to `compose.yaml`, so
// this model follows it.
suite: Lotto suite | Runs together on one computer [system]
  > The four parts of the suite, started together and wired to each other.
  @exec: | Our lottery number service
  @engineer >> `compose.yaml` in `lotto-options-core` builds the four services from source
    (`deploy/backend/Dockerfile` for the three JVM services, `deploy/web-ui/Dockerfile` for the
    website) as the `lotto-stack` project: `results-service`, `core`, `bff` and `web-ui`, each
    published on `127.0.0.1` and started in that order as each one's health check passes. The
    Results store reports healthy only once its catalog lists a game, so a first start needs the
    internet. The Results store and website sources come from sibling clones.

  website: Website | Picks a game, generates numbers [service]
    icon web
    code compose:web-ui npm:lotto-options-ui
    > The screens the user sees: the dashboard, the Smart Generator with its betslip printout,
      and range analysis (how often winning numbers fall in each band of numbers). Every request
      goes to the Screen API.
    @engineer: | lotto-options-ui, Angular 21
    @engineer >> An Angular 21 single-page app, zoneless and standalone. It calls only relative
      `/ui/*` URLs. nginx serves the built app on port 80 (published as 4200) and proxies
      `/ui/*` to `${BFF_URL}` with buffering off and a 300 s read timeout; in development
      `ng serve` with `proxy.conf.json` does the same.

  screen-api: Screen API | Prepares data for each screen [service module]
    icon api
    code compose:bff maven:lotto-options-core-bff
    > Takes what the Analysis engine returns and shapes it into exactly what one screen needs.
      Some dashboard statistics are still worked out here, and are due to move into the engine.
    @exec: Website back end | Gets each screen its data
    @engineer: BFF | lotto-options-core-bff, WebFlux :8080
    @engineer >> Spring WebFlux on `127.0.0.1:8080`, the only security edge (ADR-0002), serving
      one view model per screen (ADR-0001, ADR-0003). Endpoints under `/ui`: `ping`,
      `catalog/states`, `dashboard/{state}/{game}` (plus `/matrix`, `/position-trend`,
      `/pattern-edge`), `POST dashboard/{state}/{game}/generate`,
      `POST dashboard/{state}/{game}/betslip/pdf`, `POST range-analysis`. `CoreApiClient` calls
      five engine endpoints on `core-api.base-url` (`http://localhost:8002`). OpenAPI at
      `/v3/api-docs`, from which the website generates its types. ADR-0009 records that the
      assemblers (`FrequencyRollup`, `PositionTrend`, `PatternEdge` and others) still compute
      statistics that belong in the engine.

  engine: Analysis engine | Generates and scores picks [service]
    icon gear
    code compose:core
    > The brains of the suite: game dashboards, suggested numbers, range analysis and printable
      betslips. Only the Screen API talks to it.
    @exec: Number engine | Turns past draws into suggested picks
    @exec > Where the value is: it analyses past winning numbers and suggests numbers to play.
    @engineer: Core API | lotto-options-core-server :8002
    @engineer >> Spring Boot on port 8002, one jar (`lotto-options-core-service.jar`) built from
      the modules in the Engineering view. Health at `/api/v1/gameout/health`. Every module but
      `core-model` also depends on the Results store's `lottery-result-persistence-service-models`
      for `LotteryGame`, so `deploy/backend/Dockerfile` builds that first.

    engine-service: Engine service | Answers requests and does the work [module]
      code maven:lotto-options-core-server
      > The part of the engine that runs: it takes requests, works out dashboards and picks, and
        looks up draw history.
      @engineer: core-server | Spring Boot module
      @engineer >> Inbound REST controllers, the application services and the outbound client
        to the Results store.

      request-handling: Request handling | Receives requests from the Screen API [part]
        icon route
        code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.core.server.adapter.web.*
        code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.controller.GameOutController
        > Accepts each request and hands it to the right service.
        @engineer >> `AnalysisController` (`/api/v1/analysis`: `GET /{state}/{game}/dashboard`,
          `POST /generate/numbers`, `POST /range-analysis`), `CatalogController`
          (`GET /api/v1/catalog/states`), `GameOutController` (`/api/v1/gameout/*`) and
          `BetslipController` (`POST /api/v1/betslip/pdf`).

      // Unsure: nothing in the website or the Screen API calls `/api/v1/gameout/*`; its caller,
      // if any, is not in these repositories.
      game-analysis: Game analysis | Works out each game's statistics [part]
        icon grid
        code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.core.application.dashboard.*
        code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.core.application.range.*
        code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.core.application.betslip.*
        code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.core.application.gameout.*
        code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.core.server.adapter.pdf.*
        code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.service.impl.GameOutBacktestService
        code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.service.impl.BalanceCalculatorService
        > Builds the dashboard's statistics, the range analysis and the printable betslips from a
          game's past draws.
        @engineer >> `LotteryDashboardService` (with the `core-result-analyzer` dashboard
          analyzers), `LotteryRangeAnalysisService` and `BetslipGenerationService` (PDFBox, with
          the Texas slip templates under `betslip/Texas/`). The `GameOut*Service`s behind
          `/api/v1/gameout/*` live here too.

      pick-generation: Pick generation | Runs the chosen strategy [part]
        icon dice
        code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.core.application.generate.*
        > Gets the game's draw history, then keeps making sets of numbers with the strategy the
          user chose and checking how each would have done in past draws, for up to about 20
          seconds, until it finds a set whose target prize is due.
        @engineer >> `LotteryNumberGenerationService.getRandomNumberGeneratedData`. It loads
          history through `LotteryStateService`, picks a generator from
          `RandomNumberGeneratorFactory` by the request's `numberGenerator`, and back-tests each
          set until the `targetedPrizeLevel` has gone unwon for about as many draws as its odds
          predict, within `SearchLimits.DEFAULT` (25 million comparisons or 20 s). It returns
          `GeneratedNumberData` with prize-level results.

      history-lookup: Draw history lookup | Fetches past draws from the Results store [part]
        icon search
        code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.core.server.adapter.resultsservice.*
        code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.service.impl.WebClientLotteryStateService
        code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.service.LotteryStateService
        > How the engine gets draw data: it asks the Results store for a game's history and
          waits for the answer.
        @engineer >> `WebClientLotteryStateService`, built from `persistence-service.host`
          (`http://localhost:8001/`) and `persistence-service.games` (`api/v1/state/games`). It
          blocks on the call on a `boundedElastic` thread. The catalog comes from
          `GET api/v1/all/v2/state-games`.

    strategies: Strategies library | The number-picking methods [module]
      icon books
      code maven:lotto-options-core-result-analyzer
      > The three ways to pick numbers: Balanced Spread (copies the most common mix of low and
        high, odd and even numbers in past draws), Secure Random and Fast Random. Also the
        dashboard analyzers and the ticket scorer.
      @engineer: core-result-analyzer | Generators and scorer
      @engineer >> `LowOddLowEvenHighOddHighEvenLotteryNumberGenerator` and
        `TemplateMatrixLotteryNumberGenerator` (categorical), `UniformLotteryNumberGenerator`
        (`SecureRandom`, `ThreadLocalRandom`), the `analyzer.dashboard` analyzers, and
        `LotteryResultsComparator`.

    formats: Shared data formats | The shapes of requests and results [module]
      code maven:lotto-options-core-model
      > The definitions of what a request and a result look like, shared by the engine and the
        Screen API so both sides agree.
      @engineer: core-model | Request and result types
      @engineer >> Such as `RandomNumberGeneratorRequest` and `GeneratedNumberData`. Plain types
        with Jackson annotations and Lombok; an ArchUnit rule keeps the Results store's types
        out of it.

    // Unsure: the desktop client itself is not in these repositories, so it is not drawn. Its
    // `HttpLotteryPersistenceClient` calls `/api/v1/lottery-targeting/*`, which the Results
    // store no longer offers.
    legacy: Legacy desktop support | Kept for the old desktop app [module legacy]
      icon monitor
      code maven:lotto-options-core-legacy-desktop
      > Code kept only until the old desktop app is retired. Nothing else in the suite uses it,
        but it still reads draws from the Results store on its own.
      @engineer: core-legacy-desktop | Quarantined by ADR-0009
      @engineer >> `TicketGenerationController` (`/api/v2/generate-tickets` with its SSE status)
        and `LottoOptionsCoreController` (`/api/v1/lotto-options-core/games/*`). Packaged in the
        engine's jar at runtime scope only, so no engine class compiles against it. Deleted with
        the desktop client.

  store: Results store | Keeps each game's past draws [service]
    icon cylinder
    code compose:results-service maven:lottery-result-persistence-server
    code maven:lottery-result-aggregator maven:lottery-result-persistence-service-models
    > Downloads winning numbers, keeps each game's past draws, and answers the engine's
      questions about them.
    @exec > Keeps every game's winning numbers on file and refreshes itself every two hours.
    @engineer: Results service | lottery-result-persistence-service :8001
    @engineer >> Spring Boot on port 8001. Modules `lottery-result-persistence-server` (the API),
      `lottery-result-aggregator` (updating, reading, saving) and
      `lottery-result-persistence-service-models` (the draw types). No database: the draws are
      files.

    history-service: Draw history service | Answers requests for past draws [part]
      icon history
      code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.controller.PersistenceServiceController
      code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.service.impl.LotteryDataService
      code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.cache.CatalogCacheEvictor
      > Lists the states and games on file and returns a game's draw history on request,
        remembering recent answers until the next refresh.
      @engineer >> `PersistenceServiceController` under `/api/v1`: `GET /states`,
        `GET /states/{stateName}/games`, `GET /all/state-games`, `GET /all/v2/state-games` (the
        catalog the engine reads), `GET /state-games/{state}` and `POST /state/games`
        (`{stateName, gameName}` in, a `StateGamesResponse` holding the `LotteryGame` out).
        Answers come from six in-memory caches (`gameData`, `catalogV1`, `catalogV2`,
        `stateGameNames`, `states`, `stateGames`); `LotteryDataService` reads a `.ser` file only
        on a miss.

    updater: Results updater | Refreshes every game every 2 hours [part]
      icon refresh
      code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.agr.jobs.DrawResultJob class:com.floyd.lottoptions.agr.polling.*
      > Every two hours, and once at start-up, has every game's results refreshed: four games at
        a time, at most four downloads a second, retrying a game up to three times. A game that
        fails keeps its previous file.
      @engineer >> `DrawResultJob` on cron `0 0 */2 * * ?`, and a `CommandLineRunner` at
        start-up. `LotteryResultPollingService` runs one task per game on a 4-thread pool, each
        wrapped in resilience4j retry (3 attempts) and a rate limit of 4 a second, then publishes
        `PollCompletedEvent` with the counts of games that succeeded, had rows rejected, or
        failed, even when every game failed.

    reader: Results reader | Downloads and saves each game's results [part]
      icon document
      code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.agr.processor.* class:com.floyd.lottoptions.agr.documentreaders.*
      > Downloads a game's results file, turns its rows into draws, and saves the latest 8,000 of
        them.
      @engineer >> `TexasLotteryHistoryProcessor.processGame` downloads each game's CSV export
        with `CsvFileReader`, keeps the last `MAX_DRAWS` (8000) rows, parses them into a
        `LotteryGame` and writes it with `LotteryGameSerializer`. The 17 Texas games are listed
        under `urls.states` in `application.yml`. A Louisiana PDF reader exists, but its URLs are
        commented out there.

    saved-draws: Saved draws | One file per game [store]
      icon archive
      code class:com.floyd.lottoptions.agr.persistence.LotteryGameSerializer
      > Where the draws live: one file per game, replaced whole each time new results come in,
        so a half-written file is never read.
      @engineer >> `LotteryGameSerializer` writes one Java-serialized `LotteryGame` at
        `{lottery.storage.base-dir}/{STATE}/{Game}.ser` (temp file, fsync, then rename).
        `base-dir` is `tmp` locally and `/data` in the container, the `draw-data` volume.

texas: Texas Lottery website | Publishes winning numbers per game [external]
  > The official source of results. The suite downloads one file per game from it.
  @engineer >> Per-game CSV exports under
    `texaslottery.com/export/sites/lottery/Games/.../Winning_Numbers/`. The only live external
    source.

// ----------------------------------------------------------- relationships
// Declared once, at the finest grain. A view that shows only a container draws the sentence
// between the nearest drawn elements, so the Big picture needs no relationships of its own.

user uses website
website gets screen data from screen-api
  @exec: works through
  @engineer: calls /ui/* on
screen-api requests analysis from request-handling
  @exec: asks for numbers from
  @engineer: calls /api/v1/* on
request-handling hands requests to pick-generation
request-handling hands dashboard requests to game-analysis
pick-generation gets past draws from history-lookup
pick-generation picks and scores tickets with strategies
game-analysis gets past draws from history-lookup
// Game analysis also uses the dashboard analyzers of the strategies library. Its engineer
// paragraph says so; the line is left out so the Services view has no crossing lines.
history-lookup fetches draw history from history-service
  @engineer: POSTs /api/v1/state/games to
legacy reads draw history from history-service
  @engineer: calls /api/v1/state-games/* on
history-service reads saved-draws
updater has each game refreshed by reader
reader downloads results from texas
  @engineer: fetches CSV exports from
reader saves draws into saved-draws
  @engineer: writes {STATE}/{Game}.ser to
updater announces each finished refresh to history-service

engine-service depends on strategies, formats [code]
engine-service ships with legacy [code]
  @engineer: has a runtime-only dependency on
strategies depends on formats [code]
legacy depends on formats [code]
screen-api uses the shapes in formats [code]
  @engineer: depends on

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- views

view big-picture: Big picture
  show user, suite +1, texas
  > The suite in one line: the user uses the **website**, the website asks the **Screen API**,
    the Screen API asks the **Analysis engine**, and the engine gets its draw history from the
    **Results store**, which keeps itself current from the **Texas Lottery website**.
  > Click any part for what it does; the other views go one level deeper.

view services: Services
  show user, suite +2, texas
  > The same suite one level down: what each part contains and which parts talk to each other.
  > Requests flow from the top down. Draw data flows the other way: the **Results reader**
    downloads it and the **Draw history service** hands it to the engine.

view engineering: Engineering: code modules
  show [module]
  kind code
  @exec: How the code is organised
  > The Maven modules of **lotto-options-core** by dependency. The Screen API shares only the
    data formats with the engine.
  @engineer >> ADR-0009's target modules (`core-domain`, `core-application`,
    `core-api-contract`) do not exist yet. The groundwork is done: the legacy quarantine (S19),
    ArchUnit rules with a frozen, shrink-only baseline (S21, ADR-0010), PMD (S22) and dependency
    analysis (S23). The module split itself has not started.

// --------------------------------------------------------------- scenarios
// Steps are sentences too: the subject sends, the object receives.

scenario generate-picks: Generate picks
  > What happens when the user clicks **Generate Numbers** on the Smart Generator, step by step.
    The strategy runs in the engine; the Results store only supplies the past draws.

  user clicks Generate Numbers on website
    > The user has chosen a game, a strategy, a target prize and how many tickets.
    @engineer >> The Smart Generator screen posts a `numberGenerator`
      (`LOW_ODD_LOW_EVEN_HIGH_ODD_HIGH_EVEN`, `SecureRandom` or `ThreadLocalRandom`),
      `numberSetsPerPattern`, `targetedPrizeLevel` and `drawDaysPerWeek`.
  website asks for picks from screen-api
    > The website sends the request on; nginx passes it through without waiting for the whole
      answer.
    @engineer: POSTs /ui/dashboard/{state}/{game}/generate to
    @engineer >> Proxied to `BFF_URL` with buffering off. `DashboardController` hands it to
      `SmartGeneratorService`, which maps it to the engine's request and calls
      `CoreApiClient.generateNumbers`.
  screen-api requests analysis from request-handling
    > The Screen API asks the engine to generate numbers.
    @engineer: POSTs /api/v1/analysis/generate/numbers to
    @engineer >> With a `RandomNumberGeneratorRequest` from `core-model`.
  request-handling hands the request to pick-generation
    @engineer >> `LotteryNumberGenerationService.getRandomNumberGeneratedData`.
  pick-generation asks for past draws from history-lookup
    > Pick generation needs the game's history first.
    @engineer >> `LotteryStateService.getLottoGameDrawData(game, state)`, implemented by
      `WebClientLotteryStateService`.
  history-lookup fetches draw history from history-service
    > The Results store returns the game's past draws, from memory or from its saved file.
    @engineer >> `POST /api/v1/state/games` with `{stateName, gameName}`; a `StateGamesResponse`
      with the `LotteryGame` comes back, from the `gameData` cache when it can.
  history-service returns the draw history to history-lookup
  history-lookup hands the past draws to pick-generation
  pick-generation generates and scores tickets with strategies
    > The chosen strategy picks the numbers, and each set is checked against past draws to show
      what it would have won. This repeats, for up to about 20 seconds, until a set's target
      prize is due.
    @engineer >> The generator chosen by `numberGenerator` through
      `RandomNumberGeneratorFactory`; `LotteryResultsComparator` scores each set into
      prize-level results, within `SearchLimits.DEFAULT`.
  strategies returns scored tickets to pick-generation
  pick-generation returns the picks to request-handling
    @engineer >> A `GeneratedNumberData` with the tickets, their prize-level results and the
      estimated days until a win.
  request-handling returns the picks to screen-api
    @engineer >> 200 with `GeneratedNumberData`.
  screen-api sends picks ready to show to website
    > The Screen API reshapes the picks into exactly what the Smart Generator screen shows.
    @engineer >> `GeneratedNumbersAssembler`, called by `SmartGeneratorService`, maps the data to
      a `GeneratedNumbersViewModel`.
  website shows the picks to user

scenario results-poll: Results poll
  > How the suite keeps its draw history current: every two hours, and once at start-up, so a
    fresh install fills itself before anyone asks for a game.

  updater has each game refreshed by reader
    > Four games at a time, at most four downloads a second, each retried up to three times.
    @engineer >> 17 games, one task each, wrapped in retry and the rate limit.
  reader downloads the results file from texas
    > The game's whole results file, not just the newest draws.
  texas returns the game's results to reader
  reader saves the latest draws into saved-draws
    > The game's file is replaced whole, with its latest 8,000 draws.
    @engineer >> Parsed into a `LotteryGame` and written to `{STATE}/{Game}.ser` through a temp
      file and a rename.
  reader reports how the game went to updater
    @engineer >> A `GameProcessingResult` with the rows read and rejected.
  updater announces the finished refresh to history-service
    > The history service forgets what it had remembered, so the next request sees the new
      draws.
    @engineer >> `PollCompletedEvent`, an in-process Spring event; `CatalogCacheEvictor` clears
      all six read caches.

// ------------------------------------------------------------- walkthrough
// One story for a room: it moves through views and scenarios, and its narration adapts too.

walkthrough tour: A tour of the Lotto suite
  step big-picture: website
    > The user opens the **website** in a browser and picks a state and a game.
    @exec > Customers use the **website** to choose a game and get suggested numbers.
  step big-picture: screen-api
    > The **Screen API** prepares the data for each screen, mostly by reshaping the engine's
      answers.
  step big-picture: engine
    > The **Analysis engine** does the thinking: dashboards, generated picks, scoring and
      betslips.
    @exec > The **engine** is where the value is: it turns past draws into suggested picks.
    @engineer >> One Spring Boot jar on port 8002, built from the modules in the Engineering view.
  step services: pick-generation
    open engine-service
    > Inside the engine, **Pick generation** runs the strategy the user chose and checks each set
      against past draws until it finds one worth playing.
    @engineer >> A bounded search: 25 million comparisons or 20 s, whichever comes first.
  step big-picture: store
    > The **Results store** keeps each game's past draws and refreshes them from the Texas
      Lottery website every two hours.
    @exec > The **Results store** keeps our data current on its own, with no one to run it.
  step services: store
    > Inside the store, the updater has every game refreshed, the reader downloads and saves each
      game's results, and the history service answers the engine.
  step generate-picks
    > Now follow one request: what happens when the user clicks **Generate Numbers**.
  step big-picture
    > The whole suite again.
